  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sexual &amp; Reproductive Health Rights for YoungWomen and Adolescent Girls in Ghana</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project is the continuation of GHAB25 which has a lifespan of 7 years from April 2001 to June 2008. It seeks to increase the enjoyment of Sexual and Reproductive Health Rights by young people throughout Ghana by conducting Reproductive Health Education sessions on life skills, gender equality, Gender Based Violence, Menstruation and Menstrual Hygiene Management, Family Planning and related topics for Young Women and Adolescent Girls, Young Men and Adolescent Boys between the ages of 10 to 24years. It also aims to strengthen the health systems in Ghana by improving on the knowledge, skills and attitudes of health care service providers by organising trainings in various areas to the target groups mainly midwives, obstetricians and gynaecologists across the project implementing areas. It will also support some health facilities with basic medical equipment, commodities and essential medicines to equip them to better provide the needed services to the target population. The Project would be implemented in 5 regions (Greater Accra, Central, Bono East, Northern and North East Regions) and 8 Metropolitan, Municipal and District Assemblies (Ashaiman, Ewutu Afutu Senya East, Cape Coast, Techiman, Sene East, Sagnarigu, Savelugu and West Mamprusi). It would be implemented in partnership with 5 local Civil Society Organisations (Northern Sector Action Awareness Centre (NORSAAC), Participatory Action for Rural Development Alternatives (PARDA), Planned Parenthood Association of Ghana (PPAG), Women in Law and Development in Africa (WILDAF) and Social Enterprise Development Foundation of West Africa (SEND) Ghana). It also seeks to engage in evidence -based advocacy by training and equipping women and youth groups with the requisite skills to conduct action research to gather evidence for engagement at the national level.</narrative>
  </description>
